interconnect: qcom: sm7150: keep the QUP0 BCM alive

On the POCO X3 NFC (surya) the SoC freezes silently within seconds of the
aggregated bandwidth on the QUPv3 wrapper 0 path (qhm_qup_center, BCM
"QUP0") dropping to zero, i.e. as soon as every serial engine of the wrapper
is runtime-suspended: the Bluetooth UART closed by bluetoothd, the
touchscreen SPI, the haptics and the NFC I2C engines idle. The AOP then
gates the wrapper's core clock and the next access to any engine of that
wrapper wedges the bus. Measured on 2026-09-08 by holding engines
runtime-active one at a time: any single engine keeping its default
GENI_DEFAULT_BW vote (QUP0 vote 101) is enough for the phone to survive
Bluetooth power cycles; releasing the last one kills it within ~20 s.
Keeping the wrapper AHB clocks on (CLK_IS_CRITICAL) does not help, so it is
the RPMh-managed core clock, not the GCC branches.

Mark the BCM keepalive so the framework always votes a minimum for it, as
the memory, shub, multimedia and config-noc BCMs already are.

--- a/drivers/interconnect/qcom/sm7150.c
+++ b/drivers/interconnect/qcom/sm7150.c
@@ -1343,7 +1343,7 @@
 
 static struct qcom_icc_bcm bcm_qup0 = {
 	.name = "QUP0",
-	.keepalive = false,
+	.keepalive = true,
 	.num_nodes = 2,
 	.nodes = { &qhm_qup_center,
 		   &qhm_qup_north
